<h1>Part 1 · Read this first — the 2-minute version</h1>

<h3>1.1 What you have</h3>
<p>PRANA is a Flutter app for Android that presents itself as a health-monitoring companion paired
with a wrist-worn sensor band. It shows heart rate, blood oxygen (SpO<sub>2</sub>), body temperature,
motion, ambient temperature / humidity / air quality, sleep, a 0–100 personal risk score, plain-English
recommendations, and an emergency SOS flow with fall detection.</p>

<p>All of the <b>logic</b> for that is written and working: the maths, the personal-baseline learning,
the risk scoring, the recommendation text, the fall-detection state machine, the charts, the whole UI.</p>

<h3>1.2 What is real vs. simulated</h3>
<table>
<tr><th style="width:26%">Layer</th><th style="width:14%">Status</th><th>What that means</th></tr>
<tr><td>UI, navigation, charts</td><td><span class="tag t-real">REAL</span></td><td>Finished. 5 destinations, custom bottom bar, hand-painted gauge and charts.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Personal baseline learning</td><td><span class="tag t-real">REAL</span></td><td>Genuine rolling mean / standard-deviation statistics, persisted to disk.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Risk engine + recommendations</td><td><span class="tag t-real">REAL</span></td><td>Genuine multi-domain weighted scoring with explainable drivers.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Fall detection</td><td><span class="tag t-real">REAL</span></td><td>Genuine impact-spike-then-stillness algorithm over accelerometer magnitude.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Local storage</td><td><span class="tag t-real">REAL</span></td><td><code>shared_preferences</code>. Nothing leaves the phone.</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Sensor input</b></td><td><span class="tag t-stub">SIMULATED</span></td><td>A timer generates lifelike fake vitals every 2 seconds. <b>No Bluetooth code exists yet.</b></td></tr>
<tr><td><b>ML inference</b></td><td><span class="tag t-part">HAND-CODED</span></td><td>Six sigmoid equations written by hand in Dart. Not a trained neural network. No TensorFlow in the project.</td></tr>
<tr><td>SOS dispatch</td><td><span class="tag t-stub">SIMULATED</span></td><td>Shows a hardcoded Delhi GPS string. Sends no SMS, makes no call, reads no real GPS.</td></tr>
</table>

<p>So your job is to replace the two <span class="tag t-stub">SIMULATED</span> / <span class="tag t-part">HAND-CODED</span>
boxes with real ones. The good news is that the app was built with exactly that swap in mind — both are
hidden behind an <i>interface</i>, so nothing else in the app has to change.</p>

<h3>1.3 The only two files that matter for integration</h3>
<div class="ok"><span class="t">Your two plug points</span>
<p><b>Hardware:</b> <code>lib/features/devices/sensor_data_source.dart</code> defines an abstract class
<code>SensorDataSource</code> with four streams. Write a class that implements it and emits real Bluetooth
data, and the whole app instantly runs on hardware.</p>
<p><b>AI/ML:</b> <code>lib/features/ai/inference_service.dart</code> defines an abstract class
<code>MLInferenceService</code> with one real method, <code>runInference(List&lt;double&gt; 16 features)
→ PredictionResult (6 probabilities)</code>. Write a class that implements it using TensorFlow Lite, and
the whole app instantly runs on a real model.</p>
<p>Both are injected in one place: the <code>PranaHealthController</code> constructor, called from
<code>lib/main.dart</code>.</p>
</div>

<pre><span class="c">// lib/main.dart — this is where you swap the engine. Today:</span>
ChangeNotifierProvider(
  create: (_) =&gt; PranaHealthController(storage: storageService),
)

<span class="c">// After you finish Parts 7 and 8, it becomes:</span>
ChangeNotifierProvider(
  create: (_) =&gt; PranaHealthController(
    storage: storageService,
    customSensorSource: BluetoothSensorDataSource(),  <span class="c">// real band</span>
    customMlService:    TFLiteInferenceService(),     <span class="c">// real model</span>
  ),
)</pre>

<p class="fp">That constructor already accepts both parameters — see
<code>lib/core/services/prana_health_controller.dart</code> lines 111–115. You do not need to modify the
controller to inject them.</p>

<h1 class="pb">Part 2 · What the app actually is</h1>

<h3>2.1 The product idea</h3>
<p>A wearable band streams biometrics to the phone over Bluetooth Low Energy. The phone — <b>without any
internet connection</b> — learns what "normal" looks like <i>for this specific person</i>, runs a small
neural network locally to estimate several health risks, combines that with environmental danger
(heat, air quality), and produces one explainable number plus an action to take. If it detects a fall or
a critical reading, it starts an SOS countdown.</p>

<p>The pitch is <b>personalisation</b> plus <b>offline operation</b>. A generic app says "60–100 BPM is
normal". PRANA says "your normal is 64–78 BPM, and you are at 92 while resting, which for you is a
+18% deviation" — and it says this in a village with no mobile data during a heatwave.</p>

<h3>2.2 The five screens</h3>
<table>
<tr><th style="width:16%">Destination</th><th style="width:30%">File</th><th>What it shows</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>Home</b></td><td><code>features/dashboard/dashboard_screen.dart</code></td>
    <td>Risk gauge (0–100), the top 3 factors driving that score, the recommended action, a 2×2 grid of live vitals, an environment summary, and a scenario chip.</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Vitals</b></td><td><code>features/health/health_screen.dart</code></td>
    <td>Segmented control: Heart · Oxygen · Temp · Sleep. Line charts with your learned baseline band drawn behind them. The Sleep segment embeds <code>features/sleep/sleep_screen.dart</code>.</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>SOS</b> (centre)</td><td><code>features/emergency/emergency_screen.dart</code></td>
    <td>Not a tab — a button that <i>navigates</i> to the emergency screen. Confirm-to-send, so a pocket mis-tap costs nothing. Handles the 10-second fall countdown.</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Insights</b></td><td><code>features/insights/insights_screen.dart</code></td>
    <td>Segmented control: Analysis · Environment. Risk-domain breakdown, alert feed, and (2nd segment) <code>features/environment/environment_screen.dart</code>.</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Me</b></td><td><code>features/profile/profile_screen.dart</code></td>
    <td>Profile, learned baseline values, emergency contacts, and links to <b>Device</b>, <b>Privacy</b>, and <b>Demo Lab</b>.</td></tr>
</table>

<div class="note"><span class="t">The Demo Lab is your debugging console</span>
<p><code>lib/features/demo/demo_lab_screen.dart</code>, reached from <b>Me → Demo Lab</b>, is where every
developer tool lives: inject one of 6 disaster scenarios, trigger a fake fall, toggle mock-vs-hardware
mode, and — critically for you — <b>watch the live 16-number feature vector and the 6 model output
probabilities</b>. When you wire up real hardware or a real model, this screen is how you check it is
actually working.</p>
</div>

<h3>2.3 Why "offline-first" and "edge AI"</h3>
<p>"Offline-first" means there is no server, no API key, no login, and no network call anywhere in the
codebase. Persistence is <code>shared_preferences</code> (a small key–value store on the phone).
"Edge AI" means inference happens on the phone's CPU rather than in a cloud service — which is what
TensorFlow Lite is for, and what Part 8 sets up.</p>

<h1 class="pb">Part 3 · Getting it running</h1>

<h3>3.1 Prerequisites</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Flutter SDK 3.47.2 or newer (you have it — <code>flutter --version</code> confirms).</li>
  <li>Android SDK with platform-tools on PATH (you have it).</li>
  <li>An Android phone with USB debugging enabled, or an emulator.</li>
  <li>Java 17 (the Gradle config targets JVM 17).</li>
</ul>

<h3>3.2 First run</h3>
<pre>cd /d/sih_test_app_new
flutter pub get          <span class="c"># download packages listed in pubspec.yaml</span>
flutter analyze          <span class="c"># static check — should report only 2 pre-existing style infos</span>
flutter run -d chrome    <span class="c"># fastest way to see the UI, no Android build needed</span></pre>

<p>Chrome is the fastest feedback loop for UI work. It cannot do Bluetooth, so once you start Part 7 you
must move to the phone.</p>

<h3>3.3 Running on your phone</h3>
<pre>flutter devices                       <span class="c"># find your phone's id</span>
flutter run -d &lt;device-id&gt;            <span class="c"># hot-reload session, best for development</span>
flutter build apk --debug             <span class="c"># produces build/app/outputs/flutter-apk/app-debug.apk</span></pre>

<p>With <code>flutter run</code>, pressing <span class="kbd">r</span> hot-reloads your Dart changes in
under a second, and <span class="kbd">R</span> does a full restart. Note that <b>hot reload does not
re-run the constructor of <code>PranaHealthController</code></b> — so after you change which
sensor source or ML service is injected in <code>main.dart</code>, you need a full restart
(<span class="kbd">R</span>) or a fresh <code>flutter run</code>.</p>

<h3>3.4 The Gradle memory fix (already applied)</h3>
<div class="warn"><span class="t">Why the Android build was failing</span>
<p><code>android/gradle.properties</code> was asking the Gradle build daemon for an <b>8 GB Java heap
plus 4 GB metaspace</b>. On a 16 GB machine with only ~3 GB actually free, that Java process cannot
start, so it dies instantly and the build fails with the confusing message
<i>"A new daemon was started but could not be connected to."</i></p>
<p>It is now set to a sane size:</p>
</div>
<pre><span class="c"># android/gradle.properties (current)</span>
org.gradle.jvmargs=-Xmx2G -XX:MaxMetaspaceSize=512m -XX:ReservedCodeCacheSize=256m -XX:+HeapDumpOnOutOfMemoryError</pre>
<p>If a build still fails to start the daemon, close memory-heavy apps (browsers, Android Studio,
emulators) and then escalate in this order:</p>
<pre>cd /d/sih_test_app_new/android &amp;&amp; ./gradlew --stop   <span class="c"># kill stale daemons</span>
cd /d/sih_test_app_new &amp;&amp; flutter clean             <span class="c"># wipe build caches</span>
<span class="c"># then, in android/gradle.properties, drop to -Xmx1536m</span>
<span class="c"># last resort, add this line: org.gradle.daemon=false</span></pre>

<h3>3.5 Common failures and what they mean</h3>
<table>
<tr><th style="width:44%">Message</th><th>Cause &amp; fix</th></tr>
<tr><td><code>A new daemon was started but could not be connected to</code></td><td>Gradle heap too large for free RAM. See 3.4.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>Unable to load asset: assets/models/...</code></td><td>The path in code does not match the <code>assets:</code> block in <code>pubspec.yaml</code>, or you forgot <code>flutter pub get</code> after editing it.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>MissingPluginException</code></td><td>You added a native package (like a BLE plugin) but did not do a full restart. Stop the app completely and run <code>flutter run</code> again — hot reload cannot register new native plugins.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>RenderFlex overflowed by N pixels</code> in the console</td><td>A layout is too wide/tall for the screen. Yellow-black stripes appear in the UI. Fix by wrapping the offending child in <code>Expanded</code>/<code>Flexible</code>, or switching a <code>Row</code> to a <code>Wrap</code>.</td></tr>
<tr><td>App shows no data / gauge stuck at initial value</td><td>You are in hardware mode with no band connected. Go <b>Me → Demo Lab</b> and turn <b>mock mode</b> back on.</td></tr>
</table>

