<h3>5.2 Walking one reading through all six stages</h3>
<p>Here is the actual code, from
<code>lib/core/services/prana_health_controller.dart</code>. This one method
<i>is</i> the pipeline — read it once and you understand the app.</p>

<pre>Future&lt;void&gt; _onHealthReadingReceived(HealthReading reading) async {
  _currentHealth = reading;

  <span class="c">// ① buffer for the charts — keep the last 50 points only</span>
  _hrHistory.add(reading.heartRate);
  _spo2History.add(reading.spo2);
  _tempHistory.add(reading.bodyTemperature);
  if (_hrHistory.length &gt; 50) _hrHistory.removeAt(0);
  <span class="c">// … same for the other two …</span>

  <span class="c">// ② teach the baseline service, then ask it how abnormal this is FOR ME</span>
  baselineService.recordReading(reading);
  final hrDev     = baselineService.getHrDeviationPercent(reading.heartRate);
  final spo2Drop  = baselineService.getSpo2DropPoints(reading.spo2);
  final tempElev  = baselineService.getTempElevation(reading.bodyTemperature);

  <span class="c">// ③ build the 16-number vector the model expects</span>
  final features = FeatureProcessor.extractFeatureVector(
    health:       reading,
    environment:  _currentEnvironment,
    baseline:     baselineService.currentBaseline,
    sleep:        _currentSleep,
    hrTrendSlope: _computeHrTrendSlope(),
  );
  _lastFeatureVector = features;   <span class="c">// Demo Lab displays this</span>

  <span class="c">// ④ run the model  ← YOUR TFLITE CALL ENDS UP HERE</span>
  _lastPrediction = await mlService.runInference(features);

  <span class="c">// ⑤ turn probabilities into one explainable score</span>
  _currentRisk = riskEngine.calculateRisk(
    prediction: _lastPrediction,
    health: reading, environment: _currentEnvironment,
    hrDeviationPercent: hrDev, spo2DropPoints: spo2Drop,
    tempElevation: tempElev,
  );

  <span class="c">// ⑥ raise an alert if warranted, then repaint the UI</span>
  if (_currentRisk.level == RiskLevel.high ||
      _currentRisk.level == RiskLevel.critical) {
    _appendAlert(_currentRisk);
  }
  notifyListeners();
}</pre>

<div class="note"><span class="t">What <code>notifyListeners()</code> does</span>
<p>The controller is a <code>ChangeNotifier</code>. Screens read it with
<code>context.watch&lt;PranaHealthController&gt;()</code>. Calling <code>notifyListeners()</code>
tells Flutter "my data changed" and every watching widget rebuilds. That is the entire
UI-update mechanism — there is no other. So <b>if your new data isn't showing up on screen, the
question is always "did a <code>notifyListeners()</code> happen after I set the value?"</b></p>
</div>

<h3>5.3 The controller is the spine</h3>
<p>Stream subscriptions are set up once in <code>_bindStreams()</code>:</p>

<pre>void _bindStreams() {
  _healthSub?.cancel();  <span class="c">// cancel old ones first — critical when swapping sources</span>
  <span class="c">// …</span>
  _healthSub      = _sensorSource.healthStream.listen(_onHealthReadingReceived);
  _motionSub      = _sensorSource.motionStream.listen(_onMotionReadingReceived);
  _environmentSub = _sensorSource.environmentStream.listen(_onEnvironmentReceived);
  _deviceSub      = _sensorSource.deviceStatusStream.listen((d) {
                      _currentDevice = d; notifyListeners();
                    });
  fallDetectionService.fallAlertStream.listen((_) {
    emergencyService.triggerFallDetected();
    notifyListeners();
  });
}</pre>

<p>State the controller exposes to the UI (all read-only getters):</p>
