<h3>6.4 DeviceInfo — <code>shared/models/device_info.dart</code></h3>
<p>Fields: <code>id</code>, <code>name</code>, <code>connectionState</code>
(<code>disconnected</code>/<code>scanning</code>/<code>connecting</code>/<code>connected</code>/<code>error</code>),
<code>batteryLevel</code> (int %), <code>rssi</code> (int dBm — signal strength, −30 is excellent,
−90 is poor), <code>firmwareVersion</code>, and four booleans
<code>isHeartRateActive</code> / <code>isSpo2Active</code> / <code>isTemperatureActive</code> / <code>isMotionActive</code>.</p>

<div class="gap"><span class="t">Gap to fix later</span>
<p><code>DeviceInfo.defaultDevice()</code> hardcodes <code>batteryLevel: 88</code> and
<code>rssi: -58</code>. Until you push real values into <code>deviceStatusStream</code>, the Device
screen will always show 88% battery and −58 dBm even with a real band attached. Part 7.6 shows how to
emit the real ones.</p>
</div>

<h3>6.5 UserBaseline — <code>shared/models/user_baseline.dart</code></h3>
<p>The learned personal normal, persisted to disk:
<code>normalHrMin</code>, <code>normalHrMax</code>, <code>normalSpo2Min</code>,
<code>normalTempMin</code>, <code>normalTempMax</code>, <code>normalHrvMin</code>,
<code>sampleCount</code>, <code>lastCalibrated</code>, and <code>isCalibrated</code>
(<code>true</code> once <code>sampleCount ≥ 20</code>). Defaults come from
<code>AppConstants</code>: HR 60–100, SpO<sub>2</sub> min 95, temp 36.1–37.2, HRV min 20.</p>

<p>How it learns, from <code>features/baseline/baseline_service.dart</code>:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Only readings whose <code>activityLevel</code> contains "rest" or "low" are recorded — you don't
      want a sprint polluting your resting normal.</li>
  <li>Rolling buffers capped at 200 samples.</li>
  <li>Every 20 samples it recalibrates: <code>mean ± max(8.0, 1.645 × stdDev)</code> for HR
      (then floored at 48 and ceiled at 110 for safety), and analogous bands for SpO<sub>2</sub> and temperature.</li>
  <li>1.645 standard deviations is the 90% two-sided confidence band — i.e. "90% of your resting
      readings fall in here".</li>
</ul>

<div class="note"><span class="t">Practical consequence for hardware testing</span>
<p>When you first connect a real band, the baseline is still the generic default (HR 60–100) and
<code>isCalibrated</code> is <code>false</code>. It takes 20 resting readings to calibrate. If your
firmware never sets <code>activityLevel</code> to "Resting" or "Low", <b>the baseline will never
calibrate</b> and every deviation number will be measured against the generic default. Send
<code>"activityLevel": "Resting"</code> when the wearer is still.</p>
</div>

<h1 class="pb">Part 7 · HARDWARE INTEGRATION — connecting real sensors</h1>

<p>This is the part you asked for most. By the end of it, a real band will be streaming into the app
and the Home screen will show live temperature, SpO<sub>2</sub> and heart rate.</p>

<h3>7.1 The seam you plug into</h3>
<p>Here is the entire interface, <code>lib/features/devices/sensor_data_source.dart</code>. Anything
that satisfies this contract can drive the whole app:</p>

<pre>abstract class SensorDataSource {
  Stream&lt;HealthReading&gt;      get healthStream;
  Stream&lt;MotionReading&gt;      get motionStream;
  Stream&lt;EnvironmentReading&gt; get environmentStream;
  Stream&lt;DeviceInfo&gt;         get deviceStatusStream;

  Future&lt;void&gt; connect(String deviceId);
  Future&lt;void&gt; disconnect();
  Future&lt;void&gt; startScanning();
  Future&lt;void&gt; stopScanning();
  void dispose();
}</pre>

<p>A "Stream" in Dart is a pipe you push values into and someone else listens to. You create one with
a <code>StreamController</code>: <code>_controller.add(value)</code> pushes,
<code>_controller.stream</code> is the read end. Use <code>.broadcast()</code> so multiple listeners
are allowed.</p>

<h3>7.2 Honest gap list — what is missing today</h3>
<p>Do not skip this table. These are the eleven things that will actually bite you, in the order they
will bite.</p>

<table>
<tr><th style="width:5%">#</th><th style="width:32%">Gap</th><th>Consequence &amp; where it is fixed below</th></tr>
<tr><td>1</td><td>No Bluetooth package in <code>pubspec.yaml</code></td><td>There is literally no BLE code in the project. Fixed in <b>7.3</b>.</td></tr>
<tr><td>2</td><td><code>AndroidManifest.xml</code> has <b>zero</b> permission entries</td><td>Scanning silently returns no devices on Android 12+. Fixed in <b>7.4</b>.</td></tr>
<tr><td>3</td><td><code>connect()</code> fakes a handshake with <code>Future.delayed(600ms)</code></td><td>The UI shows "connected" while nothing is connected. Fixed in <b>7.6</b>.</td></tr>
<tr><td>4</td><td><code>startScanning()</code> / <code>stopScanning()</code> are empty method bodies</td><td>The Device screen's scan button does nothing. Fixed in <b>7.6</b>.</td></tr>
<tr><td>5</td><td><code>onTelemetryPacketReceived()</code> exists but <b>nothing ever calls it</b></td><td>The parsing path is written and correct — it just has no data feeding it. Fixed in <b>7.6</b>.</td></tr>
<tr><td>6</td><td><code>toggleMockMode(false)</code> swaps in the BLE source and rebinds streams, but never calls <code>startScanning()</code> or <code>connect()</code></td><td><b>Turning off mock mode today gives you a frozen, empty app.</b> Fixed in <b>7.7</b>.</td></tr>
<tr><td>7</td><td>The example JSON in <code>sensor_parser.dart</code>'s doc comment does <b>not match</b> the real model keys</td><td>Copying that example gives you defaults for every field. Correct contract in <b>7.5</b>.</td></tr>
<tr><td>8</td><td><code>timestamp</code> parsing + a bare <code>catch (_) {}</code></td><td>An epoch-integer timestamp throws, the empty catch swallows it, and <b>the whole packet vanishes with no error message.</b> See <b>7.5</b>.</td></tr>
<tr><td>9</td><td><code>DeviceInfo.defaultDevice()</code> hardcodes battery 88% / RSSI −58</td><td>Real battery and signal never surface. Fixed in <b>7.6</b>.</td></tr>
<tr><td>10</td><td>Default BLE packet limit is 20 bytes; the JSON contract is ~250 bytes</td><td>Packets arrive truncated → invalid JSON → silently dropped. Fixed in <b>7.6</b> via <code>requestMtu</code>.</td></tr>
<tr><td>11</td><td>Every reading <code>await</code>s inference then repaints the UI</td><td>Fine at 0.5 Hz. At 10 Hz+ you must throttle. See <b>7.9</b>.</td></tr>
