</div>

<h3>7.4 Step 2 — Android permissions and minSdk</h3>

<p><b>File:</b> <code>android/app/src/main/AndroidManifest.xml</code>. Add the permission block
<i>above</i> the <code>&lt;application&gt;</code> tag. Right now this file has none at all.</p>

<pre>&lt;manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"&gt;

    <span class="c">&lt;!-- ===== Android 12 (API 31) and newer ===== --&gt;</span>
    &lt;uses-permission android:name="android.permission.BLUETOOTH_SCAN"
                     android:usesPermissionFlags="neverForLocation" /&gt;
    &lt;uses-permission android:name="android.permission.BLUETOOTH_CONNECT" /&gt;

    <span class="c">&lt;!-- ===== Android 11 (API 30) and older ===== --&gt;</span>
    &lt;uses-permission android:name="android.permission.BLUETOOTH"
                     android:maxSdkVersion="30" /&gt;
    &lt;uses-permission android:name="android.permission.BLUETOOTH_ADMIN"
                     android:maxSdkVersion="30" /&gt;
    &lt;uses-permission android:name="android.permission.ACCESS_FINE_LOCATION"
                     android:maxSdkVersion="30" /&gt;

    <span class="c">&lt;!-- Declares the app uses BLE. required=false lets non-BLE phones install it. --&gt;</span>
    &lt;uses-feature android:name="android.hardware.bluetooth_le"
                  android:required="false" /&gt;

    &lt;application
        android:label="PRANA"          <span class="c">&lt;!-- was "sih_test_app_new" --&gt;</span>
        android:name="${applicationName}"
        android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher"&gt;
        <span class="c">&lt;!-- … leave the rest of the file untouched … --&gt;</span></pre>

<p><b>Why <code>neverForLocation</code>:</b> since Android 12, Bluetooth scanning can theoretically be
used to infer your location, so Google requires you to either request location permission or explicitly
declare that you will not derive location from scan results. Declaring <code>neverForLocation</code>
is the honest and much less intrusive choice here.</p>

<p><b>File:</b> <code>android/app/build.gradle.kts</code>. <code>flutter_blue_plus</code> requires
API 23+. Pin it explicitly rather than inheriting:</p>
<pre>defaultConfig {
    applicationId = "com.example.sih_test_app_new"
    minSdk = 23                      <span class="c">// ← was: flutter.minSdkVersion</span>
    targetSdk = flutter.targetSdkVersion
    versionCode = flutter.versionCode
    versionName = flutter.versionName
}</pre>

<p>Finally, permissions declared in the manifest are not automatically granted — on Android 6+ you must
also ask at runtime. Add this helper and call it before your first scan:</p>

<pre><span class="c">// lib/features/devices/ble_permissions.dart  (new file)</span>
import 'package:permission_handler/permission_handler.dart';

class BlePermissions {
  <span class="c">/// Returns true only if we may scan AND connect. Call before startScanning().</span>
  static Future&lt;bool&gt; request() async {
    final result = await [
      Permission.bluetoothScan,
      Permission.bluetoothConnect,
      Permission.locationWhenInUse,   <span class="c">// needed on Android ≤ 11 only</span>
    ].request();

    <span class="c">// locationWhenInUse may be permanently denied on Android 12+ and that is fine,</span>
    <span class="c">// so only the two bluetooth grants are mandatory.</span>
    return result[Permission.bluetoothScan]?.isGranted == true &amp;&amp;
           result[Permission.bluetoothConnect]?.isGranted == true;
  }
}</pre>

<h3>7.5 Step 3 — the firmware data contract, and three traps</h3>

<p>Your band should send <b>one JSON object per packet</b>, UTF-8 encoded, over a single BLE notify
characteristic. This is the exact contract, derived from the <code>fromJson</code> methods of the three
models — not from the doc comment in <code>sensor_parser.dart</code>, which is wrong:</p>

<pre>{
  "heartRate":        82.5,        <span class="c">// BPM        → HealthReading</span>
  "spo2":             97.2,        <span class="c">// %          → HealthReading</span>
  "bodyTemperature":  36.85,       <span class="c">// °C wrist   → HealthReading  ★ not "temperature"</span>
  "activityLevel":    "Moderate",  <span class="c">// Resting|Low|Moderate|High</span>
  "steps":            4120,        <span class="c">// int</span>
  "restingHeartRate": 68.0,        <span class="c">// BPM</span>
  "hrvRmssd":         45.0,        <span class="c">// ms</span>

  "accX":             0.08,        <span class="c">// g          → MotionReading</span>
  "accY":             0.14,
  "accZ":             0.98,
  "isFallSuspected":  false,       <span class="c">// bool, optional firmware hint</span>

  "temperature":      31.2,        <span class="c">// °C ambient → EnvironmentReading ★ different key!</span>
  "humidity":         54.0,        <span class="c">// %RH</span>
  "aqi":              48,          <span class="c">// int 0-500</span>
  "heatExposureHours": 1.5         <span class="c">// see 7.11</span>
}</pre>

<div class="gap"><span class="t">Trap 1 — two different temperature keys</span>
<p>Body temperature is <code>"bodyTemperature"</code>. Ambient temperature is
<code>"temperature"</code>. They coexist in the same flat packet because three different models each
pick out the keys they care about. Sending only <code>"temperature"</code> means the body-temp card
silently shows the default 36.8 °C forever while the environment card works — a bug that looks like
"the thermistor is broken".</p>
</div>

<div class="gap"><span class="t">Trap 2 — timestamp will destroy your packet</span>
<p>All three models do <code>DateTime.parse(json['timestamp'] as String)</code>. If your firmware sends
<code>"timestamp": 1724784000000</code> (a Unix epoch integer, which is the natural thing to send),
that cast throws. And because <code>onTelemetryPacketReceived</code> wraps everything in
<code>catch (_) {}</code>, the exception is swallowed and <b>the entire packet is discarded with no log
line, no error, nothing.</b> The app just looks frozen.</p>
<p><b>Either</b> omit <code>timestamp</code> entirely (the models default to
<code>DateTime.now()</code>, which is what you want anyway — the phone's clock is more trustworthy than
a microcontroller's) <b>or</b> send ISO-8601: <code>"2026-08-28T14:30:05Z"</code>.
Recommendation: omit it.</p>
</div>

<div class="gap"><span class="t">Trap 3 — the silent catch, in general</span>
<p>While you are bringing hardware up, make the failure loud. Change the catch in
<code>bluetooth_sensor_data_source.dart</code> to actually print:</p>
<pre>} catch (e, st) {
  <span class="c">// During bring-up you NEED to see malformed packets, not swallow them.</span>
  debugPrint('[PRANA/BLE] dropped packet: $e');
  debugPrint('$st');
}</pre>
<p>This single change will save you hours. A dropped packet is invisible otherwise.</p>
</div>

<p>The packet above is ~250 bytes. Keep it under ~180 bytes if you can by dropping fields your hardware
doesn't measure — every omitted key just falls back to a sensible default. A minimal useful packet is:</p>
<pre>{"heartRate":82.5,"spo2":97.2,"bodyTemperature":36.85,"activityLevel":"Resting"}</pre>
</file>

